{
  "query": "At RadiantBank your 24 TB Oracle 12c system runs on premises and supports critical transactions. You must migrate to Cloud Spanner with only about a 60 minute cutover and leadership requires a clear rollback option during go live. Which migration approach should you implement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Database Migration Service to continuously replicate from Oracle to Cloud Spanner and rely on Cloud Storage backups as the rollback plan",
      "explanation": "This suggests using Database Migration Service for change replication into Spanner and depending on object storage backups to revert if needed",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create the Cloud Spanner instance and run a bulk import that converts schema and loads data, then enable reverse replication from Spanner back to the on premises Oracle database for rollback",
      "explanation": "This option proposes a bulk load followed by replication in the opposite direction to the original Oracle environment for rollback",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use the Cloud Spanner Migration Tool to convert the Oracle schema and stream changes from Oracle to Spanner with Datastream and a Dataflow pipeline and keep Oracle as the system of record until cutover so you can fall back by routing traffic to Oracle if needed",
      "explanation": "This approach relies on schema conversion plus change data capture into Spanner while delaying the final switch until you are ready",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Import the Oracle schema and data into Firestore and create an export as the fallback plan",
      "explanation": "This choice moves the workload to Firestore and uses an export artifact as a contingency copy",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Use the Cloud Spanner Migration Tool to convert the Oracle schema and stream changes from Oracle to Spanner with Datastream and a Dataflow pipeline and keep Oracle as the system of record until cutover so you can fall back by routing traffic to Oracle if needed</b>.</p><p>This approach fits a 24 TB migration with a tight cutover window because the Spanner migration tooling converts the Oracle schema and the Datastream and Dataflow pipeline continuously replicates changes from Oracle into Spanner. You can run an initial load and then apply change data capture so Spanner stays nearly in sync. Keeping Oracle as the system of record until you are ready to flip traffic means you only need a brief write pause to let replication catch up and then you can switch application writes to Spanner. If any issues arise during go live you can immediately route traffic back to Oracle since it remained authoritative which satisfies the rollback requirement.</p><p><i>Use Database Migration Service to continuously replicate from Oracle to Cloud Spanner and rely on Cloud Storage backups as the rollback plan</i> is incorrect because Database Migration Service does not replicate from Oracle into Cloud Spanner and Cloud Storage backups do not provide a live rollback path for transactional applications during a timed cutover.</p><p><i>Create the Cloud Spanner instance and run a bulk import that converts schema and loads data, then enable reverse replication from Spanner back to the on premises Oracle database for rollback</i> is incorrect because there is no supported reverse replication from Cloud Spanner back into Oracle. A pure bulk import also risks longer downtime for a database of this size and does not provide a reliable rollback option.</p><p><i>Import the Oracle schema and data into Firestore and create an export as the fallback plan</i> is incorrect because Firestore is not a relational database and does not meet Cloud Spanner’s strong relational and transactional requirements. An export is not a practical rollback for a critical transactional cutover.</p>",
